# Defining mods with `defineMod`

A Ruby Decko mod is a directory of set modules: `set/type/basic.rb`,
`set/all/help.rb`, `set/self/help.rb`, each written in the set-module DSL
(`format :html do`, `view :core do`, `event :x, :store do`). decko-ts mods are
written in the same shape with `defineMod`
(`src/card/mod-loader/mod-dsl.ts`). It compiles to the `DeckoModManifest` the
mod loader already consumes, so a mod written with it loads, registers and
renders exactly as a hand-written manifest does.

`card-mod-help`, `card-mod-markdown`, `card-mod-session` and
`card-mod-ace_editor` are written with it; read
`src/card/mod-loader/mods/card-mod-help.ts` beside the gem's `set/` tree to see
the correspondence on a real mod.

## The shape

```ts
import { defineMod } from "../mod-dsl.js";

export default defineMod("card-mod-example", { group: "gem" }, ({ set }) => {
  set("type/basic", ({ view, format, event, setting, accessor }) => {
    view("core", (ctx) => ctx.fmt.processContent(ctx.fmt.renderRaw()));
    format("html", ({ view }) => {
      view("bar", { cache: "never", wrap: "slot" }, (ctx) => "...");
    });
    event("my_event", "integrate", { on: "update", when: "my_predicate?" }, (card) => {
      /* ... */
    });
  });
});
```

`defineMod(gemName, options?, body)` returns the manifest. The gem name is
normalized the way `Cardio::Mod.normalize_name` does it: `card-mod-example`
becomes the mod `example`, and the manifest keeps `card-mod-example` as its
gem name. A name without the `card-mod-` prefix is a deck-local mod.
`options.group` is the `Cardio::Mod::Dirs` group (`gem-card`, `gem-defaults`,
`gem`, `custom`).

## Ruby to TypeScript

| Ruby (card 1.110.0)                              | `defineMod`                                              |
|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| `set/type/basic.rb`                              | `set("type/basic", (s) => ...)`                           |
| `format :html do ... end`                        | `format("html", ({ view }) => ...)`                       |
| `format :json, :csv do ... end`                  | `format(["json", "csv"], ...)`                            |
| `format do ... end`, or `view` at set level      | `format(({ view }) => ...)`, or `view(...)` (base format) |
| `view :bar, cache: :never, wrap: :slot do ... end` | `view("bar", { cache: "never", wrap: "slot" }, fn)`     |
| `view :bar, template: :haml`                     | `view("bar", { template: "haml" }, fn)` (see below)       |
| `def custom_ok? ... end` inside a format block | `method("custom_ok?", fn)` inside that format block      |
| `before :core do ... end` (format or set level) | `before("core", fn)` (see "Before-view callbacks")       |
| `wrapper(:cream) { ... }` inside a format block | `wrapper("cream", (ctx, interior, opts) => ...)` |
| `wrapper :cream, :div, class: "creamy"` | `wrapper("cream", "div", {class: "creamy"})` |
| `event :x, :store, on: :update do ... end`       | `event("x", "store", { on: "update" }, fn)`               |
| `event :x, after: :y, when: :z? do ... end`      | `event("x", { after: "y", when: "z?" }, fn)`              |
| `event :x, :validate, changed: :name`            | `event("x", "validate", { changed: "name" }, fn)`         |
| `setting_opts group: :editing, position: 1`      | `setting({ group: "editing", position: 1 })`              |
| `card_accessor :email, type: :phrase`            | `accessor("email", { type: "phrase" })`                   |
| `card_reader :status` / `card_writer :salt`      | `reader("status")` / `writer("salt")`                     |
| `assign_type :phrase`                            | `assignType("phrase")`                                    |
| `def content ... end` (set-module override)      | `content(fn)`                                             |
| `basket[:tasks][:x] = {...}`                     | `basketTask({...})`                                       |
| `basket[:warnings] << :x`                        | `basketWarning("x")`                                      |
| `set_pattern/tab.rb`                             | `setPattern("tab", spec, { index })` (mod level)          |
| boot-time rule warm (`Rule::Cache`)              | `prepareEngine(async (host) => ...)` (mod level)          |

Symbols are written as strings. Every call appends one entry to the
manifest in the order it is made, which is the order Ruby reads the file in;
the loader then walks mods, set patterns and set files in
`Cardio::Mod::Dirs` order, so the order of `set()` calls within a mod does
not change load order. Calling `set()` twice with the same path reopens the
same set file, as reopening a Ruby module does.

### Views

A view body is a `ViewFn`: `(ctx) => string | number | null`, where `ctx.fmt`
is the format (Ruby's `self` in a view block) and `ctx.voo` its view options.
The view options are Ruby's view settings: `unknown`, `perms`, `cache`,
`wrap`, `denial`, and `template`.

`template: "haml"` records that the Ruby view renders from a HAML template
beside its set file. The engine has no HAML interpreter, so a template view
still supplies its body as a function: the port of the template. The option
is kept so the port can be checked against the template it transcribes.

### Before-view callbacks

`before(view, fn)`, inside a format block or at set level (base format),
is Ruby's `before :view do ... end`: it declares the format method
`_before_<view>`. Before a view renders, the format looks that method up
through the card's set and format chain and calls it, as Ruby's
`before_view` does; the requested view's hook also runs when an unknown
card renders a substituted view. The callback receives the view context, so
it can change `ctx.voo` before the body runs. `card-mod-session` uses it for
its `before :core`.

### Format helper methods

Each render engine owns a copy of the compiled fallback views. Bind the
intended `ModLoader` to `engine.modLoader` for set-scoped dispatch. A tool
that deliberately exercises flat registration passes `engine.views` as the
loader constructor's second argument before loading it. Loading or resetting
another deck never updates that engine's flat table.

Inside a format block, `method(name, fn)` declares a synchronous helper
without adding a view. Its manifest entry is `formats[format].methods`,
separate from `views`. A later declaration reopens that method in its set
and format; the normal set/format lookup applies, including inherited base
methods. A view or another helper calls it as
`ctx.fmt.callFormatMethod("name", ...args)`.

```ts
format("html", ({method, view}) => {
  method("custom_ok?", (ctx) => ctx.fmt.card.name.s === "Allowed Example");
  view("custom_controls", {perms: "custom_ok?", denial: "blank"}, () => "...");
});
```

Permission predicates receive the active view context. False, null and
undefined deny; zero, empty strings, arrays and objects are truthy as in
Ruby. They are evaluated on each gate, including a repeated render. Native
async declarations are rejected during load. A mistyped helper returning
a promise is refused synchronously, and an eventual rejection is reported
without becoming an unhandled process rejection. Helpers do not receive an
implicit asynchronous preparation stage.

This call surface covers declared helpers and permission predicates. It
does not make arbitrary Ruby method names override TypeScript renderer
internals. Undeclared format helpers raise `NoMethodError`; their diagnostic
messages describe the TypeScript declaration gap rather than a Ruby receiver.
The broader Ruby method/DSL contract remains under comparison.

### Declared custom wrappers

Function wrappers generate scoped `_wrapper_<name>` and `wrap_with_<name>`
format methods. A view's `wrap` setting selects them; the loader wraps the
resolved view and its super chain once, using the current card/format's
independent setting lookup. Wrapper options are shallow copies, as in the
native invocation method.

```ts
format("html", ({wrapper, view}) => {
  wrapper("cream", (_ctx, interior) => `cream_${interior}_cream`);
  view("cream_cake", {wrap: "cream"}, () => "cake");
});
```

A wrapper can call declared format helpers through `ctx.fmt.callFormatMethod`.
Call the generated wrapper explicitly with
`ctx.fmt.callFormatMethod("wrap_with_cream", "cake", options)`.
Multiple names nest from right to left; a hash supplies options per name.
An ordered list can mix names and `[name, options]` tuples, for example
`wrap: ["cherry", ["choc", {class: "white"}]]`. Generated methods also accept
a synchronous content callback in place of the content argument.
Body-only overrides keep inherited wrap settings, and `wrap: {}` clears them.
Reopening the private wrapper method changes subsequent calls without replacing
the view body. Declared wrapper methods remain private to their owning loader.

Existing translated mods keep their explicit built-in wrapping. A newly
wrapper-declaring mod refuses an unknown wrapper with `ArgumentError`; legacy
unresolved metadata is not reclassified as native wrapper support.

Tag declarations use `wrapper("cream", "div", {class: "creamy"})`. With a
nonblank default class, invocation classes precede default classes and duplicates
are removed by Decko's class merge. Without one, ActionView-style class-array
filtering removes false/blank entries and retains duplicate names. Default
attributes are overridden shallowly, and caller options remain unchanged. JSON
data escapes HTML-sensitive characters and both Unicode line separators before
attribute escaping; scalar-string data retains ordinary attribute escaping.
Interior markup is preserved; attribute values are escaped. The portable object,
ARIA and class shapes and their representation limits follow below.

### Portable tag attribute values

Tag declarations accept `ModTagOptions`, exported from `mod-manifest.ts`.
Its recursive `ModTagValue` contains strings, finite numbers, booleans, null,
readonly arrays and plain JSON objects. Function-wrapper options retain their
separate application-defined surface.

```ts
import type { ModTagOptions } from "../mod-manifest.js";

const defaults: ModTagOptions = {
  title: { section: "notes" },
  class: { active: true, disabled: false },
  aria: { describedby: ["summary", { details: true }] },
};
wrapper("notes", "section", defaults);
```

Ordinary object attributes join their key/value entries in insertion order.
Nested arrays/objects use the pinned Ruby inspection spelling; null ordinary
entry values preserve an empty joined position. Object keys follow the existing
JSON-to-Ruby Symbol-key adapter. This does not represent arbitrary Ruby hash
key classes or standalone Symbol values.

Class objects and compound ARIA arrays/objects use ActionView token selection:
false/null values omit conditional keys, while zero, empty strings, arrays and
objects are truthy for those keys. Scalar blank tokens are omitted, nested arrays
are traversed, and an empty ARIA token result emits no attribute. Order and
duplicates follow the original helper. With a nonblank default class, Decko's
separate class merge stringifies objects and removes equal duplicate values;
it does not apply conditional-key selection to those merged objects.

Defaults are overridden shallowly. Ordinary arrays join flattened values;
JSON data keeps its existing JSON.stringify representation and numeric/HTML escaping. Caller
options remain unchanged. Existing numeric transport limits still apply:
JavaScript numbers do not encode Ruby Integer/Float identity or arbitrary precision.

Outside the existing data serializer, custom classes (including Date, Map, Regexp and SafeBuffer equivalents),
functions, symbol values/keys, bigint, undefined attribute values, non-finite
numbers, sparse arrays, cycles and accessor-bearing nested objects are refused
with `UnsupportedModTagAttributeError`. Invalid UTF-16 strings and unassigned
Unicode characters requiring Ruby inspection also refuse. Portable arrays must
have every own index and no extra named, noncanonical or symbol keys; an extra
property cannot mask a missing index.

The `data` object keeps the earlier per-entry JSON serializer contract rather
than ordinary attribute validation. JSON.stringify alone invokes `toJSON`, with
its original receiver/key, omission rules and errors. No validation pass calls
it, even for overridden defaults. Nested custom serializers and Date inputs
therefore retain their existing JavaScript JSON behavior; this does not claim
native Ruby custom-class parity. Sparse arrays inside JSON data retain JSON's
null-hole behavior. Ordinary/class/ARIA arrays still require dense indices.

The tag overload infers this context for concrete inline declarations.
`ModTagDataAttributes` names the existing serializer's unknown-input contract,
and `ModTagOptions<{data: ModTagDataAttributes}>` annotates typed data defaults.
Legacy dynamic readonly records retain their prior call surface and runtime
validation; concrete ordinary attributes retain portable value checks.

Layout declarations, wrapper-method super and full Rails/mod registration
remain separate unsupported scope.

### Events

`event(name, stage?, options?, body?)` takes the stage positionally, as Ruby
does. The stages are Ruby's director stages (`initialize`,
`prepare_to_validate`, `validate`, `prepare_to_store`, `store`, `finalize`,
`integrate`, `after_integrate`, ...). Options:

- `on`: `"create"`, `"update"`, `"delete"` or `"save"`, or a list of them.
- `before` / `after`: the name of another event at the same stage. As in
  Ruby, an event placed relative to another takes that event's stage, so
  it is declared without one.
- `when`: the name of a predicate, such as `"codename?"`. Ruby also accepts a
  proc; native TypeScript mods supply a function, such as
  `when: card => card.name.startsWith("Public ")`. A function receives exactly
  the current card and its result is awaited. False, null and undefined skip
  the event; zero, an empty string and empty containers remain truthy, as in
  Ruby. Condition errors propagate. Names resolve at fire time against the
  deck's predicate table (`defaultDeckEventPredicates` in `default-mod-deck.ts`);
  only own declared table entries participate. A name that nothing declares
  raises when the event is considered; JavaScript prototype methods do not
  supply undeclared conditions.
- `changed` / `changing`: the card fields whose change gates the event.
- `skip: "allowed"`, `trigger: "required"`, `priority`.
- `engineOwned: true`: the event's behaviour is implemented in the engine's
  own save path; the declaration records Ruby's event list with an empty
  body, and passing a body is an error.

The body receives the acting card (Ruby's `self`), the engine host, and the
event context. An event declared without a body is recorded with an empty
one; say at the call site why, as the ported mods do.

### Accessors

`accessor`, `reader` and `writer` record Ruby's trait declarations on the
set file. The loader collects them in load order with the set path and mod
name (`ModLoader.cardAccessorDeclarations()`). Resolving a trait to its
`+name` child card is the caller's job: the declarations do not yet generate
`<name>_card` methods the way Ruby's `card_accessor` does.

## Worked example: a word-count mod

A deck wants every card to offer a `word_count` view, and wants a note when
a Basic card's content grows past a limit. In Ruby this would be a mod
`card-mod-word_count` with two set files:

```ruby
# set/all/word_count.rb
format :html do
  view :word_count, cache: :never do
    count = card.content.split.size
    %(<span class="word-count">#{count} words</span>)
  end
end

# set/type/basic.rb
event :note_long_content, :integrate, on: :save, changed: :db_content,
                                      when: :long_content? do
  Rails.logger.info "#{name} is over the word limit"
end
```

The TypeScript mod:

```ts
// card-mod-word_count.ts, a new file in src/card/mod-loader/mods/
import { defineMod } from "../mod-dsl.js";
import type { ModEventCard } from "../mod-manifest.js";

export const WORD_LIMIT = 1000;

const wordCount = (text: string): number => text.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;

/** The `when: :long_content?` predicate. */
export const wordCountModEventPredicates = {
  "long_content?": (card: ModEventCard): boolean => wordCount(card.dbContent ?? "") > WORD_LIMIT,
} as const;

export default defineMod("card-mod-word_count", { group: "custom" }, ({ set }) => {
  // set/all/word_count.rb
  set("all/word_count", ({ format }) => {
    format("html", ({ view }) => {
      view("word_count", { cache: "never" }, (ctx) => {
        const count = wordCount(ctx.fmt.renderRaw());
        return `<span class="word-count">${count} words</span>`;
      });
    });
  });

  // set/type/basic.rb
  set("type/basic", ({ event }) => {
    event(
      "note_long_content",
      "integrate",
      { on: "save", changed: "db_content", when: "long_content?" },
      (card) => {
        console.info(`${card.name} is over the word limit`);
      },
    );
  });
});
```

What each part becomes:

1. `set("all/word_count", ...)` is the set module `All::WordCount`; the view
   is registered for every card's HTML format with `cache: "never"`.
2. `set("type/basic", ...)` is `Type::Basic`; the event is recorded at the
   `integrate` stage for creates and updates that change the content, and
   runs only when `long_content?` answers true.
3. The predicate table is the port of the `long_content?` card method. Add it
   to `defaultDeckEventPredicates` in `default-mod-deck.ts` so the deck's
   event runtime can resolve the name.

To load the mod, give it to a `ModLoader` with the rest of the deck. A
deck-local mod goes in a deck mod directory, after the gem mods:

```ts
// a deck file beside card-mod-word_count.ts in src/card/mod-loader/mods/
import { ModLoader } from "../../mod-loader.js";
import { defaultDeckSources, portedBundleOrder } from "../default-mod-deck.js";
import cardModWordCount from "./card-mod-word_count.js";

const loader = new ModLoader([...defaultDeckSources(), { manifest: cardModWordCount }]);
loader.resolveOrder({
  gemMods: portedBundleOrder(),
  deckModDirs: [{ listing: ["word_count"] }],
});
loader.load();
```

A gem mod is listed in `MANIFESTS` and `ORACLE_BUNDLE_ORDER` in
`default-mod-deck.ts` instead, at its position in the bundle.

### Set modules bind through codenames

Ruby keys a set module by the anchor's CODENAME, not its name
(Pattern::Base#module_key over anchor_codenames; the port is
`anchoredModuleCandidates` in `src/card/render/set-keys-to-modules.ts`,
which also ports the consequence: an anchor whose card exists WITHOUT a
codename builds NO module key, so the set module never applies). A card
created through the write path carries no codename, and codenames are
seed data: in Ruby a mod's data/real.yml gives its own cards theirs. A
new deck-local mod therefore cannot aim a `type/<x>` or `self/<x>` set at
cards the deck created through the write path; the mod-provided view
those sections need is ANCHORLESS (`set("all/<name>")`), reachable from
every card and selected in the nest syntax (`{{<card>|<name>}}`), which
is the word-count example above. No deck-local mod ships with the port.
The composed default deck loads a deck-local mod only when the deployment
names it in `DECKO_DECK_LOCAL_MODS`
(`src/card/mod-loader/deployment-deck-local-mods.ts`, described in
`docs/handover/DEPLOYMENT.md`).

## Checking a mod

- `ModLoader.load()` returns a report of the set modules, views and settings
  it registered; `setModules()`, `viewConfigFor(view)`, `eventRegistry()` and
  `cardAccessorDeclarations()` show where each registration landed.
- `tests/card/mod-loader/mod-dsl.test.ts` pins what each DSL call compiles to.
- A mod that ports a Ruby mod is held to Ruby's bytes: its tests compare
  against output captured from Ruby Decko, and the reference harness grades
  the whole deck (`reference-harness/SPEC.md`). Converting a hand-written
  manifest to `defineMod` must leave both unchanged; the four converted mods
  were checked that way (their existing tests unchanged, synthetic-v1
  rendering 1754/1754, measured 2026-10-14 with
  `node reference-harness/runner/run.mjs --adapter
  reference-harness/adapters/ts/adapter.json --fixture
  reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1 --out tmp/g` followed by
  `node reference-harness/runner/grade.mjs --fixture
  reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1 --actual tmp/g`).
